# Every lumpy demand that stays within one whole quantum, beside whether its residues come back in
# order. One row per layer in layers/decomposed.sqlc that does not cross a whole quantum.
#
# What it asserts
#   Within one whole quantum the number of whole quanta does not change, so the residue rises with
#   the demand, and the three residues are in the same order as the three demands. Across a whole
#   quantum there is no such promise, which is the case layers/decomposed.sqlc names with
#   `crosses_tooth`.
#
# Why the crossing and not the order
#   Residues in order do not mean the demand stayed within one whole quantum: a demand of 0.2, 1.5
#   and 2.7 with a quantum of 1 gives residues of 0.2, 0.5 and 0.7, and crosses two. So the census
#   counts crossings, and this law holds the one direction that is true.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `sawtooth`. Work out `crosses_tooth` in layers/decomposed.sqlc from
#   `d_low` and `d_mode` only. A demand that stays within one whole quantum up to its most likely
#   value and crosses after it joins this population, and fails.
-- layers/decomposed.sqlc, kept to demands within one whole quantum.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT x.filing || ' / ' || x.layer AS subject,
           x.d_low_residue <= x.d_mode_residue AND x.d_mode_residue <= x.d_high_residue AS holds,
           format('demand [%s, %s, %s] inside whole quantum %s of %s, residues [%s, %s, %s]',
                  x.d_low, x.d_mode, x.d_high, x.d_low_tooth, x.q,
                  x.d_low_residue, x.d_mode_residue, x.d_high_residue) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(layers/decomposed.sqlc)
    ) x
    WHERE NOT x.crosses_tooth
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'sawtooth'
